WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.950
Same state. Same year.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.950
In one part of California, unemployment runs nearly five times the rate in another.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.950
Every community has its own economy, and its own needs.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.950
Fire crews in one region. Chip technicians in another.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.950
California has 45 local workforce development boards, covering all 58 counties.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.950
Each one is appointed by local elected officials and led by business, with labor and community members serving alongside.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.950
Every decision starts with a person looking for work, and an employer ready to hire.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.950
CWA and local boards work to unlock opportunity and prosperity.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.950
Unlock every region, and you unlock California.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.950
Local boards put federal, state, and local dollars to work on the region's goals,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.950
so they reach people as training and hiring.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.950
For California's local boards:

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.950
Coffee with employers before the doors open.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.950
A layoff notice on Friday. Staff on site Monday.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.950
A forklift certificate. Then a first paycheck.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.950
A public vote on next year's dollars.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.950
And every region adds its own.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.950
The economic impact: nearly 50,000 Californians who left these programs earned $1.26 billion the following year.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.950
That is $3.72 in wages for every federal Title I dollar local boards received.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.950
No two boards make the same choices. Every one reports on the same federal measures.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.950
That is the work we carry to Washington, and Sacramento.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:35.950
For Washington.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.950
In California alone, one local labor force is 35,000 people. Another is 2.1 million.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.950
Across 50 states, the differences multiply.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.950
The strength of the federal workforce system is its structure:

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.950
local business and community leaders decide how to invest.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.950
Fund Title I at no less than fiscal year 2026 levels.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.950
Reauthorize WIOA in a way that trusts local business and community leaders.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.950
Starting in 2027, millions of Medi-Cal enrollees must document 80 hours a month of work or other qualifying activity.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.950
Fund local boards to help them meet that requirement.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.950
For Sacramento.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.950
The state sets shared goals. Local boards and local elected officials decide how each economy gets there.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.950
Fund local boards.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.950
Hold them to results.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.950
The California Workforce Association.
